{
  "nbformat": 4,
  "nbformat_minor": 5,
  "metadata": {
    "kernelspec": {
      "name": "maxima",
      "display_name": "Maxima",
      "language": "maxima"
    },
    "aximar": {}
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Tolerance Stack Mathematics\n\nSymbolic derivations for the formulas used in `docs/02-methods.md`.\n\nThe point of this notebook is that the numbers in a tolerance analysis should not be\nmagic. In particular, Scholz's table of *distribution inflation factors* (ISSTECH-95-030,\nFigure 6) is normally copied around as a list of decimals with no visible origin. It is\nderived here from first principles in §3, and every value is reproduced exactly.\n\n**Contents**\n\n1. Variance propagation through a linear tolerance chain\n2. Worst case versus RSS\n3. Distribution inflation factors — deriving Scholz Figure 6\n4. The trapezoidal family, and why uniform and triangular are its endpoints\n5. Greenwood–Chase: mean shifts interpolate between worst case and RSS\n6. The worked example from `docs/02` §10\n7. When correlation helps and when it hurts\n\nReference: Scholz, F. (1995), *Tolerance Stack Analysis Methods*, Boeing ISSTECH-95-030.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 0. Primer — everything you need before starting\n\nYou do not need to have read Scholz to follow this notebook. Here is the whole setup.\n\n### The situation\n\nYou have an assembly. Some clearance in it matters — a lid must close, a plug must fit an\naperture, a board must not rattle. Call that clearance $G$, the **assembly criterion**. It is\nnot a dimension you can control directly; it is whatever is left over after several part\ndimensions have added and subtracted.\n\nWrite the part dimensions $L_1, \\dots, L_n$. Each has a **nominal** value $\\lambda_i$ (what\nyou drew in CAD) and an **actual** value that differs from it, because nothing is made\nperfectly. Then\n\n$$G = L_1 - L_2 - L_3 - \\cdots$$\n\nwith a $+$ for each dimension that opens the gap and a $-$ for each that closes it. That\nsigned list is the **tolerance chain**. Getting it right — a path that closes, with correct\nsigns — is the part that needs engineering judgement; everything after it is arithmetic.\n\n### What $T$ means, and the one convention that causes all the trouble\n\n$T_i$ is the **tolerance** of dimension $i$: the half-width of the permitted band, so the\ndimension is allowed to be anywhere in $\\lambda_i \\pm T_i$.\n\nBut a tolerance is a *permission*, not a *description*. To do statistics you need to know how\nthe dimension is actually distributed inside that band. The universal convention is:\n\n$$T_i = 3\\sigma_i$$\n\nthat is, the tolerance band is assumed to be the $\\pm 3\\sigma$ range of a normal distribution\ncentred on nominal. About 99.73% of parts fall inside it.\n\n**Almost every complication in this notebook comes from that convention being wrong in some\nspecific way**, and each section is a repair for one of the ways:\n\n| The assumption | How it fails | Repaired in |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dimensions vary independently | Same machine, same batch ⇒ they move together | §1, §7 |\n| $T = 3\\sigma$ | People often quote $T$ meaning $2\\sigma$ | §6 (Bender) |\n| The distribution is normal | Tool wear ⇒ uniform; pooled batches ⇒ bimodal | §3, §4 |\n| The process is centred on nominal | Centring is hard, and FDM is badly off-centre | §5 |\n\n### The two classical answers\n\n**Worst case.** Assume every dimension sits at its least favourable limit simultaneously. Add\nthe tolerances. This *guarantees* the parts fit — no distributional assumption at all — but\nit is very pessimistic, because all $n$ dimensions being extreme in the same unhelpful\ndirection at once is wildly unlikely.\n\n**RSS (root sum square).** Treat the dimensions as independent random variables and add their\n*variances* rather than their tolerances, which means the tolerances combine in quadrature.\nMuch tighter, but it buys that tightness with four assumptions, and in practice real\nassemblies vary more than RSS predicts.\n\nScholz's summary is the one to keep: these two *\"provide conservative and optimistic\nbenchmarks, respectively\"*. Neither is the truth. The truth is somewhere between, and the\njob of the rest of this notebook is to work out where.\n\n### Notation\n\n| Symbol | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| $L_i$ | actual value of dimension $i$ (a random variable) |\n| $\\lambda_i$ | nominal value of dimension $i$ |\n| $\\sigma_i$ | standard deviation of dimension $i$ |\n| $T_i$ | tolerance half-width of dimension $i$; conventionally $3\\sigma_i$ |\n| $G, \\gamma$ | assembly gap, and its nominal value |\n| $a_i$ | sensitivity: how much $G$ changes per unit change in $L_i$. Here $\\pm 1$ |\n| $\\rho_{ij}$ | correlation between dimensions $i$ and $j$ |\n| $c_i$ | inflation factor correcting for a non-normal distribution (§3) |\n| $\\eta_i$ | mean shift as a fraction of tolerance (§5) |\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 1. Variance propagation through a linear tolerance chain\n\nAn assembly criterion $G$ is a function of part dimensions $L_1, \\dots, L_n$. Linearised\nabout nominal,\n\n$$G \\approx a_0 + \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i L_i$$\n\nwhere $a_i$ is the partial derivative of $G$ with respect to $L_i$, evaluated at nominal.\n\nFor a pure length stack every $a_i = \\pm 1$: $+1$ if the dimension adds to the gap, $-1$\nif it subtracts.\n\nWriting $a$ for the sensitivity vector and $\\Sigma$ for the covariance matrix of the\ndimensions, the exact variance of $G$ is\n\n$$Var(G) = a^{T} \\, \\Sigma \\, a$$\n\nEverything else in tolerance analysis is a special case of this one expression. Let us\nexpand it and see what falls out.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* The covariance matrix of the part dimensions, shown for n = 3.\n   Σ[i,j] = ρ[i,j] σ[i] σ[j],  with ρ[i,i] = 1.                       */\n\nload(linearalgebra)$\nn : 3$\n\nΣ : genmatrix(\n      lambda([i,j], if i = j then σ[i]^2 else ρ[i,j]*σ[i]*σ[j]),\n      n, n);\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 26,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\ifx\\endpmatrix\\undefined\\pmatrix{\\else\\begin{pmatrix}\\fi \\sigma_{1}^2&\\sigma_{1}\\,\\rho_{1,2}\\,\\sigma_{2}&\\sigma_{1}\\,\\rho_{1,3}\\,\\sigma_{3}\\cr\\sigma_{1}\\,\\sigma_{2}\\,\\rho_{2,1}&\\sigma_{2}^2&\\sigma_{2}\\,\\rho_{2,3}\\,\\sigma_{3}\\cr \\sigma_{1}\\,\\sigma_{3}\\,\\rho_{3,1}&\\sigma_{2}\\,\\sigma_{3}\\,\\rho_{3,2}&\\sigma_{3}^2\\cr \\ifx\\endpmatrix\\undefined}\\else\\end{pmatrix}\\fi "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 26,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* `a` was bound to a matrix above, so a[i] would return a row.\n   Clear it and use plain indexed symbols.                              */\nkill(a)$\n\n/* Expand aᵀ Σ a as a double sum, imposing ρ[j,i] = ρ[i,j]. */\nρsym(i,j) := if i = j then 1 elseif i < j then ρ[i,j] else ρ[j,i]$\n\nVarG : expand( sum( sum( a[i]*a[j]*ρsym(i,j)*σ[i]*σ[j], j, 1, n), i, 1, n) );\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 27,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "a_{3}^2\\,\\sigma_{3}^2+2\\,a_{2}\\,\\sigma_{2}\\,\\rho_{2,3}\\,a_{3}\\,\\sigma_{3}+2\\,a_{1}\\,\\sigma_{1}\\,\\rho_{1,3}\\,a_{3}\\,\\sigma_{3}+a_{2}^2\\,\\sigma_{2}^2+2\\,a_{1}\\,\\sigma_{1}\\,\\rho_{1,2}\\,a_{2}\\,\\sigma_{2}+a_{1}^2\\,\\sigma_{1}^2"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 27,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Setting every ρ = 0 recovers the classical RSS variance.\n   This is the ONLY thing that separates RSS from the truth.            */\n\nindep : makelist(makelist(ρ[i,j] = 0, j, i+1, n), i, 1, n-1)$\nindep : flatten(indep)$\n\nVarG_indep : expand( subst(indep, VarG) );\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 28,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "a_{3}^2\\,\\sigma_{3}^2+a_{2}^2\\,\\sigma_{2}^2+a_{1}^2\\,\\sigma_{1}^2"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 28,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 2. Worst case versus RSS\n\nWith $T_i = 3\\sigma_i$ the two classical formulas are\n\n$$T_{arith} = \\sum_i \\lvert a_i \\rvert \\, T_i$$\n\n$$T_{stat} = \\sqrt{\\sum_i a_i^2 T_i^2}$$\n\nScholz's \"Pythagorean shortcut\": worst case walks the three edges of a box, RSS takes the\ndiagonal. The diagonal is always shorter, and the gap widens as terms are added — which is\nthe whole economic argument for statistical tolerancing.\n\nThe ratio for $n$ equal tolerances is worth having explicitly, because it tells you exactly\nhow much you are claiming.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* n identical terms, each with tolerance T and |a| = 1. */\nassume(T > 0, m > 0)$\n\nwc(m)  := m*T$\nrss(m) := sqrt(m*T^2)$\n\n/* The ratio is simply √n : RSS claims a factor-√n reduction over worst case. */\nratio : radcan( wc(m)/rss(m) );\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 29,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\sqrt{m}"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 29,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 3. Distribution inflation factors — deriving Scholz Figure 6\n\nRSS assumes each dimension is normal with $T = 3\\sigma$. When it is not, the contribution\nis corrected by an **inflation factor** $c$:\n\n$$T_{stat}(c) = \\sqrt{\\sum_i (c_i \\, a_i \\, T_i)^2}$$\n\nThe definition of $c$ is the key, and it is disarmingly simple. The normal convention\nidentifies the tolerance half-width with three standard deviations. So for any distribution\nsupported on $[-T, T]$ with standard deviation $\\sigma$, the equivalent \"three sigma\"\nhalf-width is $3\\sigma$, and\n\n$$c = \\frac{3\\sigma}{T}$$\n\nFor the normal case $\\sigma = T/3$ and $c = 1$, as it must be.\n\nSo every entry in Scholz's Figure 6 is just $3\\sigma/T$ for the density in question. Let us\ncompute them all. Each density below is symmetric about zero, so the mean vanishes and\n\n$$\\sigma^2 = \\int_{-T}^{T} x^2 f(x) \\, dx = 2 \\int_{0}^{T} x^2 f(x) \\, dx$$\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Helper. Every density here is symmetric about 0 on [-T,T], so we supply\n   the density on the half-interval [0,T] and double the integrals.\n\n   Returns [total area (must be 1), σ², c = 3σ/T].                       */\n\nassume(T > 0)$\n\ninfl(fhalf) := block([area, σ2],\n    area : ratsimp( 2*integrate(fhalf, x, 0, T) ),\n    σ2   : ratsimp( 2*integrate(x^2*fhalf, x, 0, T) ),\n    [area, σ2, radcan(3*sqrt(σ2)/T)] )$\n\n/* --- Uniform:  f = 1/(2T) --------------------------------------------- */\nuni : infl( 1/(2*T) );\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 30,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\left[ 1 , {{T^2}\\over{3}} , \\sqrt{3} \\right] "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 30,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Triangular:  f = (T - |x|)/T²  ------------------------------------ */\ntri : infl( (T - x)/T^2 );\n\n/* --- Elliptical / semicircular:  f = 2√(T²-x²)/(πT²) ------------------- */\nell : infl( 2*sqrt(T^2 - x^2)/(%pi*T^2) );\n\n/* --- Half cosine wave:  f = (π/4T)·cos(πx/2T) -------------------------- */\ncos_ : infl( (%pi/(4*T))*cos(%pi*x/(2*T)) );\n\n[tri, ell, cos_];\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 31,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "[1,T^2/6,sqrt(3)/sqrt(2)]\n[1,T^2/4,3/2]\n[1,((%pi^2-8)*T^2)/%pi^2,(3*sqrt(%pi^2-8))/%pi]"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\left[ \\left[ 1 , {{T^2}\\over{6}} , {{\\sqrt{3}}\\over{\\sqrt{2}}} \\right]  ,\\left[ 1 , {{T^2}\\over{4}} , {{3}\\over{2}} \\right]  , \\left[ 1 , {{\\left(\\pi^2-8\\right)\\,T^2}\\over{\\pi^2}} , {{3\\,\\sqrt{\\pi^2-8}}\\over{\\pi}} \\right]\\right] "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 31,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Symmetric beta family:  f ∝ (1 - (x/T)²)^(α-1)  on [-T,T] ---------\n   Substituting t = (x/T)² turns both integrals into beta functions, so the\n   whole family comes out in one closed form rather than case by case.   */\n\nassume(α > 0)$\n\nnum : (T^3/2) * beta(3/2, α)$      /* ∫ x² f  (unnormalised)  */\nden : (T/2)   * beta(1/2, α)$      /* ∫ f     (unnormalised)  */\n\n/* Γ(α+3/2) = (α+1/2)·Γ(α+1/2), so the ratio collapses.\n   minfactorial() applies exactly that recurrence.                       */\nσ2_beta : ratsimp( minfactorial( makefact( makegamma(num/den) ) ) );\nc_beta  : radcan( 3*sqrt(σ2_beta)/T );\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 32,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "T^2/(2*alpha+1)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "{{3}\\over{\\sqrt{2\\,\\alpha+1}}}"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 32,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Assemble the table and check every entry against Scholz Figure 6.\n   Columns: name | c (exact) | c (numeric) | Scholz | match?              */\n\ncb(A) := subst(α = A, c_beta)$\n\nrows : [\n  [\"normal (T = 3σ)\",        1,              1.000 ],\n  [\"beta  α=β=3\",            cb(3),          1.134 ],\n  [\"triangular\",             tri[3],         1.225 ],\n  [\"half cosine wave\",       cos_[3],        1.306 ],\n  [\"beta  α=β=2 (parabolic)\",cb(2),          1.342 ],\n  [\"elliptical\",             ell[3],         1.500 ],\n  [\"uniform\",                uni[3],         1.732 ],\n  [\"beta  α=β=0.6 (bimodal)\",cb(0.6),        2.023 ]\n]$\n\ncheck(r) := block([exact : r[2], num : float(r[2]), ref : r[3]],\n    [ r[1], exact, num, ref,\n      if abs(num - ref) < 0.001 then \"OK\" else \"MISMATCH\" ])$\n\ntable : map(check, rows)$\nfor row in table do print(row)$\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 33,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "[\"normal (T = 3σ)\",1,1.0,1.0,\"OK\"]\n[\"beta  α=β=3\",3/sqrt(7),1.1338934190276815,1.134,\"OK\"]\n[\"triangular\",sqrt(3)/sqrt(2),1.224744871391589,1.225,\"OK\"]\n[\"half cosine wave\",(3*sqrt(%pi^2-8))/%pi,1.3057085347625175,1.306,\"OK\"]\n[\"beta  α=β=2 (parabolic)\",3/sqrt(5),1.3416407864998738,1.342,\"OK\"]\n[\"elliptical\",3/2,1.5,1.5,\"OK\"]\n[\"uniform\",sqrt(3),1.7320508075688772,1.732,\"OK\"]\n[\"beta  α=β=0.6 (bimodal)\",2.0225995873897262,2.0225995873897262,2.023,\"OK\"]"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Every entry reproduces Scholz's Figure 6 exactly. The table is not a set of empirical\nconstants — it is $3\\sigma/T$ evaluated for eight densities.\n\nTwo things worth extracting from the derivation:\n\n- The **uniform** value $\\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732$ is the practical worst case among symmetric\n  unimodal densities on a fixed interval: uniform has the largest possible $\\sigma$ of that\n  class. A process drifting monotonically across a build — tool wear, a warming enclosure,\n  a spool running down — produces something close to it.\n- The **bimodal** beta ($\\alpha = \\beta = 0.6$) exceeds 2. Bimodality is what you get from\n  two cavities of a mould, two print sessions pooled, or two suppliers, and it inflates the\n  contribution by more than the whole worst-case-versus-RSS saving for a short chain. If you\n  pool two characterisation campaigns without checking, this is what you are silently doing.\n\n## 4. The trapezoidal family\n\nThe trapezoidal density is uniform on $[-kT, kT]$ and falls linearly to zero at $\\pm T$.\nIt has $k$ as a shape parameter interpolating between the two most important cases, so it\nis the one to reach for when you want a single tunable model rather than a menu.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Trapezoidal on [-T,T]: flat at height h on [-kT, kT], linear to 0 at ±T.\n   Integrate the half-interval in two pieces and double.                 */\n\nassume(k > 0, k < 1)$\n\nh : 1/(T*(1+k))$                         /* normalising height */\n\nflat : integrate(h, x, 0, k*T)$\nramp : integrate(h*(T-x)/(T-k*T), x, k*T, T)$\narea_trap : ratsimp( 2*(flat + ramp) );   /* must be 1 */\n\nσ2_flat : integrate(x^2*h, x, 0, k*T)$\nσ2_ramp : integrate(x^2*h*(T-x)/(T-k*T), x, k*T, T)$\nσ2_trap : ratsimp( 2*(σ2_flat + σ2_ramp) );\n\nc_trap  : radcan( 3*sqrt(σ2_trap)/T );\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 34,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "1\n(T^2*k^2+T^2)/6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "{{\\sqrt{3}\\,\\sqrt{k^2+1}}\\over{\\sqrt{2}}}"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 34,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Endpoints and the Scholz Figure 6 entry (k = 0.5). */\n\nendpoints : [\n  [\"k → 0  (triangular)\", radcan(limit(c_trap, k, 0, plus)), float(sqrt(3/2))],\n  [\"k = 0.5 (Scholz)\",    subst(k = 1/2, c_trap),            1.369          ],\n  [\"k → 1  (uniform)\",    radcan(limit(c_trap, k, 1, minus)), float(sqrt(3))]\n]$\n\nfor r in endpoints do\n    print(r[1], \" : exact = \", r[2], \" numeric = \", float(r[2]), \" expected = \", r[3])$\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 35,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "k → 0  (triangular)  : exact =  sqrt(3)/sqrt(2)  numeric =  1.224744871391589\n                    expected =  1.224744871391589\nk = 0.5 (Scholz)  : exact =  (sqrt(3)*sqrt(5))/2^(3/2)  numeric =\n                1.369306393762915  expected =  1.369\nk → 1  (uniform)  : exact =  sqrt(3)  numeric =  1.7320508075688772\n                 expected =  1.7320508075688772"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "plot2d(c_trap, [k, 0, 1],\n       [xlabel, \"k  (plateau half-width as a fraction of T)\"],\n       [ylabel, \"inflation factor c\"],\n       [legend, false],\n       [title, \"Trapezoidal family: triangular (k=0) to uniform (k=1)\"])$\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 36,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
