    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 5. Greenwood–Chase: mean shifts interpolate between the two classical methods\n\nReal processes are not centred on nominal. Let $\\Delta_i$ be the mean shift of dimension $i$\nand $\\eta_i = \\lvert \\Delta_i \\rvert / T_i$ its size as a fraction of the tolerance. The\nGreenwood–Chase (1987) model stacks the shifts **arithmetically** and the residual random\nvariation **statistically**:\n\n$$T_{assy}(\\eta) = \\sum_i \\eta_i \\lvert a_i \\rvert T_i + \\sqrt{\\sum_i \\left[ (1 - \\eta_i) a_i T_i \\right]^2}$$\n\nThe claim that this \"unifies\" worst case and RSS is easy to check: it should collapse to RSS\nat $\\eta = 0$ and to worst case at $\\eta = 1$.\n\nThis matters for FDM because $\\eta$ is genuinely large — bias, not spread, is the dominant\nerror term (see `docs/04` §1). Reading off where you sit on this curve is the honest way to\nchoose a method.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Greenwood–Chase with a common η across all terms.\n   Ts is a list of tolerances; every |a_i| = 1 here.                     */\n\ngc(Ts, η) := η*apply(\"+\", Ts) + sqrt( apply(\"+\", map(lambda([t], ((1-η)*t)^2), Ts)) )$\n\nwcase(Ts) := apply(\"+\", Ts)$\nrsse(Ts)  := sqrt( apply(\"+\", map(lambda([t], t^2), Ts)) )$\n\n/* The four tolerances from the worked example in docs/02 §10. */\nTs : [0.30, 0.20, 0.16, 0.20]$\n\n[ [\"η = 0  gives RSS      \", gc(Ts,0), rsse(Ts)],\n  [\"η = 1  gives worst case\", gc(Ts,1), wcase(Ts)] ];\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 37,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\left[ \\left[ \\mbox{ η = 0  gives RSS       } , 0.4422668877499196 ,0.4422668877499196 \\right]  , \\left[ \\mbox{ η = 1  gives worst case } ,0.8600000000000001 , 0.8600000000000001 \\right]  \\right] "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 37,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* How the assembly tolerance grows as the process drifts off nominal.\n   γ = 0.40 mm is the nominal gap in the worked example: where the curve\n   crosses it, the design starts failing.                                */\n\nplot2d([gc(Ts, η), 0.40], [η, 0, 1],\n       [xlabel, \"η  = mean shift as a fraction of tolerance\"],\n       [ylabel, \"assembly tolerance T_assy  (mm)\"],\n       [legend, \"Greenwood-Chase\", \"nominal gap γ = 0.40\"],\n       [title, \"Worst case and RSS are the two endpoints of one family\"])$\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 38,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
