    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The curve is the honest answer to \"which method should I use?\". You are not choosing between\ntwo rival theories — you are choosing a point on a line, and $\\eta$ is what fixes it.\n\nFor a well-calibrated printer whose bias you have measured and compensated, $\\eta$ might be\n0.1–0.2 and you sit near the RSS end. For an uncharacterised printer where the bias could be\nanything up to the tolerance, $\\eta \\approx 1$ and worst case is simply correct. **Measuring\nyour process is what buys you the right to move left along this curve**, and the horizontal\ndistance you gain is worth roughly a factor of two in assembly tolerance.\n\n## 6. The worked example from `docs/02` §10\n\nA PCB retained between a printed boss and a printed lid rib. The gap $G$ is the residual\nclearance; $G < 0$ means the lid will not close.\n\n| $i$ | Dimension | $a_i$ | $\\lambda_i$ | $T_i$ |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Enclosure internal height | $+1$ | 10.00 | 0.30 |\n| 2 | Boss height | $-1$ | 5.00 | 0.20 |\n| 3 | PCB thickness | $-1$ | 1.60 | 0.16 |\n| 4 | Lid rib height | $-1$ | 3.00 | 0.20 |\n\nEverything below re-derives the numbers quoted in the prose document, so the two cannot\ndrift apart.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "λs : [10.00, 5.00, 1.60, 3.00]$\nas : [   +1,   -1,   -1,   -1]$\nTs : [ 0.30, 0.20, 0.16, 0.20]$\n\nγ : apply(\"+\", map(\"*\", as, λs));            /* nominal gap */\n\n/* Standard normal CDF, for turning a z-score into a failure probability. */\nΦ(z) := float( (1 + erf(z/sqrt(2)))/2 )$\n\naT : map(\"*\", map(abs, as), Ts)$             /* |a_i| T_i */\n\nT_wc  : apply(\"+\", aT);\nT_rss : float( sqrt( apply(\"+\", map(lambda([u], u^2), aT)) ) );\n\nσ_G      : T_rss/3$\nP_fail   : Φ(-γ/σ_G);\none_in_N : round(1/P_fail);\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 39,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "0.3999999999999999\n0.8600000000000001\n0.4422668877499196\n0.0033309007877438956"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "300"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 39,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Contribution ranking — the most actionable output of any stack-up.\n   Because it is a SQUARED measure it concentrates hard: a term with twice\n   the tolerance of another contributes four times the variance.\n\n   (Note: avoid a bare \"%\" inside a Maxima string — it is read as an\n    output-line reference.)                                              */\n\nnames : [\"enclosure height\", \"boss height\", \"PCB thickness\", \"lid rib height\"]$\ntot   : apply(\"+\", map(lambda([u], u^2), aT))$\nshare : map(lambda([u], float(100*u^2/tot)), aT)$\n\nfor i thru 4 do\n    print( names[i], \" : \", share[i], \" percent of variance\" )$\n\n/* Bender's inflation factor: the correction applied when a quoted T is\n   really a 2σ figure rather than the 3σ that RSS assumes. */\nT_bender : 1.5 * T_rss;\nP_bender : Φ(-γ/(T_bender/3));\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 40,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "enclosure height  :  46.0122699386503  percent of variance\nboss height  :  20.449897750511248  percent of variance\nPCB thickness  :  13.087934560327197  percent of variance\nlid rib height  :  20.449897750511248  percent of variance\n0.6634003316248794"
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
