    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## 7. When correlation helps, and when it hurts\n\nBack to §1. The cross term in the variance is\n\n$$2 \\sum_{i<j} a_i \\, a_j \\, \\rho_{ij} \\, \\sigma_i \\, \\sigma_j$$\n\nFor positively correlated dimensions ($\\rho > 0$) the sign of each cross term is the sign of\n$a_i a_j$. So:\n\n- **same sign in the chain** ($a_i a_j > 0$) — variance **increases**, correlation hurts\n- **opposite sign** ($a_i a_j < 0$) — variance **decreases**, correlation helps\n\nRSS assumes $\\rho = 0$ throughout, so it does not merely err on one side. **It is blind, and\nit can be wrong in either direction.**\n\nThis matters enormously for 3D printing, where features on one part share a first-layer\noffset, a flow calibration and a thermal history, so $\\rho$ is routinely 0.5 or more. Two\ncases from `docs/02` §10 make the point.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* General correlated variance for a chain.\n   corr(i,j) supplies ρ; σ is taken as T/3 throughout.                    */\n\nvarG(avec, Tvec, corr) := block([m : length(avec), σv, s],\n    σv : map(lambda([t], t/3), Tvec),\n    s  : 0,\n    for i thru m do\n      for j thru m do\n        s : s + avec[i]*avec[j]*corr(i,j)*σv[i]*σv[j],\n    float(s))$\n\nindep_corr(i,j) := if i = j then 1 else 0$\n\n/* CASE A — the lid/boss chain. Printed Z dimensions are 1, 2 and 4;\n   the PCB (3) is independent. ρ = 0.6 among the printed ones.           */\ncaseA(i,j) := if i = j then 1\n              elseif i = 3 or j = 3 then 0\n              else 0.6$\n\nvA_indep : varG(as, Ts, indep_corr)$\nvA_corr  : varG(as, Ts, caseA)$\n\nprint(\"Case A  σ_G independent =\", float(sqrt(vA_indep)),\n      \"  P(G<0) =\", Φ(-γ/sqrt(vA_indep)))$\nprint(\"Case A  σ_G correlated  =\", float(sqrt(vA_corr)),\n      \"  P(G<0) =\", Φ(-γ/sqrt(vA_corr)))$\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 41,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Case A  σ_G independent = 0.14742229591663988   P(G<0) = 0.0033309007877438956\nCase A  σ_G correlated  = 0.10519822558706333   P(G<0) = 7.1666324683195e-5"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* CASE B — four printed spacers in series inside a printed cavity.\n   Now EVERY spacer enters with a = -1, so all pairwise products are +1\n   and the correlation accumulates instead of cancelling.                */\n\nasB : [   +1,   -1,   -1,   -1,   -1]$\nTsB : [ 0.30, 0.20, 0.20, 0.20, 0.20]$\nγB  : 21.00 - 4*5.00$\n\ncaseB(i,j) := if i = j then 1\n              elseif i = 1 or j = 1 then 0      /* cavity independent */\n              else 0.6$                          /* spacers correlated */\n\nvB_indep : varG(asB, TsB, indep_corr)$\nvB_corr  : varG(asB, TsB, caseB)$\n\nprint(\"Case B  σ_G independent =\", float(sqrt(vB_indep)),\n      \"  3σ =\", float(3*sqrt(vB_indep)),\n      \"  P(G<0) =\", Φ(-γB/sqrt(vB_indep)))$\nprint(\"Case B  σ_G correlated  =\", float(sqrt(vB_corr)),\n      \"  3σ =\", float(3*sqrt(vB_corr)),\n      \"  P(G<0) =\", Φ(-γB/sqrt(vB_corr)))$\nprint(\"Worst case bound        =\", apply(\"+\", map(abs, map(\"*\", asB, TsB))))$\nprint(\"Failure rate ratio      =\", float(Φ(-γB/sqrt(vB_corr))/Φ(-γB/sqrt(vB_indep))))$\n",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 42,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Case B  σ_G independent = 0.16666666666666669   3σ = 0.5   P(G<0) =\n                         9.865876449133282e-10\nCase B  σ_G correlated  = 0.2444949442785633   3σ = 0.7334848328356899\n                           P(G<0) = 2.1562697195443015e-5\nWorst case bound        = 1.0999999999999999\nFailure rate ratio      = 21855.83542082295"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "### Reading the two cases\n\n**Case A — correlation helped.** The failure rate fell from 1-in-300 to about 1-in-14,000.\nThe reason is physical and obvious once seen: the cavity height enters the chain with $+1$\nwhile the boss and rib enter with $-1$. If the printer runs everything tall, the cavity grows\n*and* the boss and rib grow, and the errors largely cancel. RSS could not see this, because\nit had already assumed the dimensions were unrelated.\n\n**Case B — correlation hurt.** Four printed spacers in series, all entering with $-1$. Now a\ncommon bias accumulates instead of cancelling. The effective assembly tolerance moves from\n0.50 (RSS) most of the way to 1.10 (worst case), and the failure rate rises by a factor of\nabout **22,000**. Nothing about the parts changed — only the assumption.\n\n### What to take from this\n\n1. **RSS is not conservative and it is not optimistic. It is blind.** It can be wrong by four\n   orders of magnitude in the failure rate, in either direction.\n2. **The sign structure of your chain determines which way.** A chain that alternates signs\n   is protected by correlation; a chain of same-sign terms is punished by it.\n3. **This is the argument for Monte Carlo with explicit correlation groups** over any\n   closed-form method, for any assembly where several dimensions come off the same machine in\n   the same session — i.e. essentially every 3D-printed assembly.\n\nYou do not need an accurate $\\rho$. Running the analysis at $\\rho = 0$, $0.5$ and $1$ and\nseeing whether the decision changes is worth far more than a precise value, and usually takes\none line.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n| Section | Result | Use it when |\n|---|---|---|\n| §1 | $Var(G) = a^{T} \\Sigma a$ | Always — everything else is a special case |\n| §2 | Worst case / RSS $= \\sqrt{n}$ for equal terms | Sizing up how much RSS is claiming |\n| §3 | $c = 3\\sigma/T$ reproduces all of Scholz Figure 6 | A dimension is not normally distributed |\n| §4 | $c = \\sqrt{3(1+k^2)/2}$ for the trapezoidal family | You want one tunable shape, not a menu |\n| §5 | Greenwood–Chase interpolates worst case ↔ RSS | The process is off-centre — i.e. FDM |\n| §6 | Numbers matching `docs/02` §10 | Checking the prose has not drifted |\n| §7 | Correlation moves the answer either way | Several dimensions share a cause |\n\n**The practical hierarchy**, in decreasing order of how much it changes your answer:\n\n1. Are the input tolerances real, or guesses? (Nothing else matters if they are guesses.)\n2. Is the process centred? (§5 — the biggest correction for FDM, and the one you can remove\n   by measuring and compensating.)\n3. Are the dimensions correlated? (§7 — orders of magnitude, in either direction.)\n4. Are the distributions normal? (§3–4 — tens of percent.)\n5. Worst case or RSS? (§2 — a factor of $\\sqrt{n}$, and the one everyone argues about.)\n\nMost discussion of tolerance analysis is about item 5. Almost all of the error is in items\n1 to 3.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}